# What theory holds the QALY up?
prompt: "Why is the standard gamble tied to expected utility theory? Which assumptions must hold for QALYs to represent preferences — utility independence, constant proportional trade-off, risk neutrality over life years? Is the QALY meant to measure welfare, or health as a social goal (welfarism versus extra-welfarism)?"

## The gamble as a ruler
level: advanced
use: decision_tree
with:
    rollback: true
    unit: Utility
    decimals: 1
    table: false
    root:
      id: choice
      type: decision
      label: Choose
      children: [{label: Certain, node: {id: sure, type: terminal, label: State q, payoff: 0.7}}, {label: Gamble, node: {id: gamble, type: chance, label: Gamble, children: [{label: Win, probability: 0.3, node: {id: full, type: terminal, label: Full health, payoff: 1}}, {label: Lose, node: {id: dead, type: terminal, label: Death, payoff: 0}}]}}]
sources:
    - id: vnm
      title: Theory of Games and Economic Behavior
      authors: von Neumann & Morgenstern
      year: 1944
      finding: axioms under which choices maximise expected utility

    scratch eu work [{"tex":"EU = p \\cdot 1 + (1-p) \\cdot 0"},{"tex":"= p"}] x 780 y 570 font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "The gamble as a ruler"

    hide best pruned value_choice

What theory lets us read a QALY weight as a person's utility — what is holding that number up?
    draw node_choice label_choice

The easy answer: a weight is just a score for how good a state feels.
    draw edge_choice_sure branchlabel_choice_sure edge_choice_gamble branchlabel_choice_gamble parallel true

Expected utility theory makes a much stronger claim, and the standard gamble shows it.
    highlight node_choice

One branch is certain: live out your life in the treated state.
    draw node_sure label_sure

The other is a gamble: full health with probability p, immediate death otherwise.
    draw node_gamble label_gamble edge_gamble_full branchlabel_gamble_full node_full label_full payoff_full edge_gamble_dead branchlabel_gamble_dead node_dead label_dead payoff_dead

We raise p until the person cannot choose between them — say, at p = 0.7.
    animate root.children.1.node.children.0.probability 0.7 duration 4

Why should that indifference point be the weight?

    pause 1.8

Von Neumann and Morgenstern showed that if choices obey a few axioms, a gamble is worth the probability-weighted average of its outco (@draw eu_box eu_line_1 ends@)mes' utilities.

Full health is fixed at 1 and death at 0, so the gamble is worth p times 1 plus one minus p times 0 — exactly p.
    draw eu_line_2 value_gamble

Indifference means the certain state is worth the same: q = 0.7.
    draw payoff_sure

    erase eu

Pause and drag p: the gamble's value follows it one for one.
    explore

The catch is that many people overweight a sure thing, and then p is no longer their utility.
    highlight edge_choice_sure node_sure label_sure

So the weight is a utility exactly as far as the person chooses by expected utility.
    highlight value_gamble payoff_sure

## Adding up rectangles
level: advanced
use: qaly_profiles
with:
    x_label: Years t
    y_label: Quality q
    x_max: 10.5
    lines: straight
    shade_between: null
    profiles:
      - {id: treated, label: Treated, color: '#2f6b8f', fill: true, waypoints: [{t: 0, u: 0.7}, {t: 10, u: 0.7}], death_at: 5}
      - {id: full, label: Full health, color: '#b5482e', fill: true, waypoints: [{t: 0, u: 1}, {t: 10, u: 1}], death_at: 3.5}
sources:
    - id: psw
      title: Utility functions for life years and health status
      authors: Pliskin, Shepard & Weinstein
      year: 1980
      finding: conditions under which QALYs represent preferences

    text area5 "3.5 QALYs" #2f6b8f at.data [2.5,0.2] font_size 30 hidden true
    text area10 "7 QALYs" #2f6b8f at.data [8.5,0.2] font_size 30 hidden true
    arrow gap5 #8f887c from.data [5,0.85] to.data [3.5,0.85] hidden true
    label gap5_l "1.5 y = 30%" above attach_to gap5 hidden true
    arrow gap10 #8f887c from.data [10,0.85] to.data [7,0.85] hidden true
    label gap10_l "3 y = 30%" above attach_to gap10 hidden true
    scratch form work [{"tex":"U(q,t) = V(q)\\cdot f(t)"},{"tex":"f(t) = t \\;\\Rightarrow\\; U = V(q)\\cdot t"}] x 300 y 330 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Adding up rectangles"

Now that one weight is a utility, can we simply multiply it by years and add?
    draw axes full_health_line

Pliskin, Shepard and Weinstein asked exactly that in 1980, and answered with conditions on preferences.
    highlight axes

Take the treated patient: five years at q = 0.7 — an area of 3.5 QALYs.
    draw curve_treated label_treated fill_treated area5

The QALY model says this is as good as 3.5 years in full health: equal areas, equal value.
    draw curve_full label_full fill_full

First condition, mutual utility independence: how you rank lengths of life does not depend on the health state, and how you rank states does not depend on the length.
    blocking false

    point at.data [5,0] gesture tap

    pause 1.2

    point at.data [0,0.7] gesture tap

    pause 4

Second, constant proportional trade-off: suppose the patient would give up 1.5 of the 5 years — 30 per cent — to be in full health.
    draw gap5 gap5_l

Now make it ten years at 0.7. How many would they give up?
    keep curve_treated curve_full

    pause 1.8

Constant proportional trade-off says 30 per cent again: three years, leaving seven in full health.
    animate profiles.0.death_at 10 profiles.1.death_at 7 duration 3

    erase area5

The share traded is fixed whatever the duration — that is what makes q a constant multiplier.
    draw area10 gap10 gap10_l

For some states people will bear a few years but not many, and then the share is not constant.
    highlight gap10_l

    erase curve_treated_ghost curve_full_ghost gap5 gap5_l gap10 gap10_l

Together these two give utility as a quality weight times some function of years;
    draw form_box form_line_1

a third condition makes that function the years themselves.
    draw form_line_2

So adding up rectangles is not arithmetic: it is an assumption about how people trade time for quality.
    highlight form_line_2

## Risk neutral about years
level: advanced
use: decision_tree
with:
    rollback: true
    unit: QALYs
    decimals: 1
    table: false
    root:
      id: choice
      type: decision
      label: q = 0.7
      children: [{label: Sure, node: {id: sure, type: terminal, label: 5 years, payoff: 3.5}}, {label: Coin flip, node: {id: flip, type: chance, label: Flip, children: [{label: Heads, probability: 0.5, node: {id: ten, type: terminal, label: 10 years, payoff: 7}}, {label: Tails, node: {id: dead, type: terminal, label: Death, payoff: 0}}]}}]
sources:
    - id: bwj97
      title: Characterizing QALYs by risk neutrality
      authors: Bleichrodt, Wakker & Johannesson
      year: 1997
      finding: for chronic states, risk neutrality in life years plus the zero condition implies the QALY model

    scratch bwj work ["BWJ 1997",{"tex":"\\text{risk neutral} \\Rightarrow \\text{QALY}"}] x 790 y 580 font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "Risk neutral about years"

    hide best pruned value_choice

The third condition is the strongest: risk neutrality over life years.
    draw node_choice label_choice

Here is the test, all in the treated state, q = 0.7: five years for sure.
    draw edge_choice_sure branchlabel_choice_sure node_sure label_sure

Or a coin flip: ten years with probability one half, immediate death otherwise.
    draw edge_choice_flip branchlabel_choice_flip node_flip label_flip edge_flip_ten branchlabel_flip_ten node_ten label_ten edge_flip_dead branchlabel_flip_dead node_dead label_dead

The QALY model scores the sure branch at 3.5 QALYs.
    draw payoff_sure

And the flip? Half of 7 plus half of 0 is 3.5 — exactly the same.
    draw payoff_ten payoff_dead value_flip

So the QALY model says you should not care which you get. Would you really flip that coin?
    highlight payoff_sure value_flip

    pause 1.8

Most people would not: studies usually find people risk averse about the length of their life.
    highlight edge_choice_sure node_sure label_sure

For them a QALY is not their utility — it rates the gamble as highly as the sure five years they prefer.
    point at.ref value_flip gesture underline

Bleichrodt, Wakker and Johannesson showed in 1997 how much hangs on this one condition.
    draw bwj_box bwj_line_1

For chronic states, risk neutrality over life years — plus the zero condition, that zero years are worth the same in every health state — is enough on its own; the other conditions follow.
    draw bwj_line_2

Pause and drag the flip's probability: its value moves, the sure branch stays at 3.5, and they meet only at one half.
    explore

    erase bwj

So the QALY as a utility stands or falls with one testable attitude to risk — and few people quite hold it.
    highlight branchlabel_flip_ten branchlabel_flip_dead

## Welfare or health?
level: advanced

    text head_w "Welfarism" #b5482e x 460 y 600 font_size 40 hidden true
    text head_x "Extra-welfarism" #2f6b8f x 775 y 600 font_size 40 hidden true
    path rule #8f887c points [[50,560],[950,560]] hidden true
    path divider dashed #8f887c points [[615,630],[615,265]] hidden true
    text row_counts "Counts" #8f887c x 170 y 500 font_size 30 hidden true
    text row_measure "Tool" #8f887c x 170 y 400 font_size 30 hidden true
    text row_needs "Needs" #8f887c x 170 y 300 font_size 30 hidden true
    text w_counts "Own utility" #b5482e x 460 y 500 font_size 36 hidden true
    text x_counts "Health" #2f6b8f x 775 y 500 font_size 36 hidden true
    text w_measure "CBA · WTP" #b5482e x 460 y 400 font_size 36 hidden true
    text x_measure "CEA · QALY" #2f6b8f x 775 y 400 font_size 36 hidden true
    text w_needs "EU conditions" #b5482e x 460 y 300 font_size 36 hidden true
    text x_needs "Agreed measure" #2f6b8f x 775 y 300 font_size 36 hidden true
    group rowlabels members ["row_counts","row_measure","row_needs"] hidden true
    scratch ex work [{"tex":"\\frac{\\Delta C}{\\Delta Q} = \\frac{30{,}000}{1.5} = 20{,}000 \\text{ vs } \\lambda"}] x 615 y 205 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Welfare or health?"

Now that the utility foundation looks thin, ask what the QALY is for.
    draw rule divider rowlabels

Two answers split health economics. The first, welfarism: what counts is each person's own utility, as they judge it.
    draw head_w w_counts

Its natural tool is cost–benefit analysis, with benefits valued by willingness to pay.
    draw w_measure

For a welfarist, the conditions of this lecture are essential: a QALY that is not a utility does not measure welfare.
    draw w_needs

The second answer is extra-welfarism, a term associated with Anthony Culyer, drawing on Amartya Sen.
    draw head_x

Here health itself is the social goal a decision-maker pursues, and the QALY measures health — not anyone's whole welfare.
    draw x_counts x_measure

Does the QALY then still need risk neutrality and the rest?
    highlight w_needs

    pause 1.8

Much less: it must be a good, agreed measure of health, not a perfect model of each person's choices.
    draw x_needs

Our example reads cleanly this way: 1.5 QALYs gained for 30,000 more — 20,000 per QALY, set against the payer's threshold λ.
    draw ex_box ex_line_1

The price is that the value judgements come into the open: the decision-maker, not the individual, chooses what counts.
    highlight x_counts

    erase ex

So the QALY rests on one of two things: expected utility theory, if it is to be a utility, or a social choice to maximise health, if it is not.
    highlight row_needs w_needs x_needs effect underline

Now you can see what an argument about QALYs is often really about — which of those two foundations the speaker stands on.
    highlight head_w head_x effect underline

